INT. MUSEUM OF CLOCKS - NIGHT

Hundreds of clocks ticking out of step. Moonlight on brass.

Four figures in black stand before a glass case holding a jewelled pocket watch.

MADAME ODETTE FONTAINE-ABARA, 50s, elegant, gloves on, studies the lock.

AUGUST "GUS" RUDZINSKI, 30s, enormous, holds a duffel. He sweats.

LI WEI-NAKAMURA, 20s, crouches at the alarm box, tools laid out like surgical instruments.

THEO BRANDT, 40s, nervous, glances at the corridor.

LI
Thirty seconds.

ODETTE
Take twenty-nine.

She taps the glass. He nods without looking up.

THEO
The guard comes at two.

GUS
It's one fifty-eight.

THEO
Which clock?

GUS
All of them.

They all look around. The clocks disagree by hours.

ODETTE
Quiet.

The alarm box clicks. A green light.

LI
Done.

She lifts the glass. He lowers the watch into the duffel with enormous tenderness.

Footsteps. A flashlight sweeps the doorway.

All four freeze. He holds his breath. She extinguishes her torch. They duck behind the case as one.

The beam passes. A cough. The footsteps retreat.

Odette exhales. Raises two fingers. They slip out, one by one, into the night.
